    "summary": "First‑time classics ignite a high‑energy Stanford show",
    "blurb": "The set is packed with debut performances – They Love Each Other, Loose Lucy and the inaugural Eyes Of The World – all delivered at peak intensity. Jerry’s guitar shines on El Paso and the extended Eyes jam, while Phil’s bass anchors the seamless Truckin' → Eyes → China Doll transition. Soundboard recordings capture the band in crisp detail, making this a must‑listen for 1973 fans.",
    "review": "Feb 9 1973 at Roscoe Maples Pavilion finds the Dead unveiling a flood of new material with confidence and fire. The first set rolls through classics before launching into fresh territory: a smoking Loose Lucy, an electrifying El Paso and the debut of They Love Each Other that immediately signals the band’s evolving palette. The second set is where the show truly erupts – Truckin' slides straight into a monumental Eyes Of The World, which stretches into an 18‑minute exploratory journey before resolving into a seamless China Doll finish. Highlights continue with tight renditions of Here Comes Sunshine, Big River and a joyous Sugar Magnolia that caps a night of high‑octane improvisation. Across the board, Jerry’s “prime” tone and inventive solos, Phil’s clear, driving bass, Bob’s solid rhythm work, Keith’s tasteful keys when they surface, and Bill’s punchy kick drum combine to make this Stanford concert one of 1973’s most memorable recordings.",
